  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nSurface high pressure progresses east of the Ohio Valley tonight \nbringing an end to the stretch of cool and dry conditions from this \npast week. Warm and humid air returns on southerly flow west of the \ndeparting high to start the week while upper level northwesterly \nflow persists. A couple periods of showers and storms are possible \nto start the week. \n\nThe first chance for showers and storms is on Sunday night. A \ncluster of storms/MCS may form in the Great Lakes region Sunday \nevening before diving south overnight. The best instability remains \nto our west which would likely favor any storms that do form diving \nsouth into Indiana and Illinois out of Michigan. However, the \npresence of some instability over our area allows at least a low end \nchance for precipitation. The severe risk with any storms Sunday \nnight is marginal since MLCAPE should be fairly limited under a \nnocturnal inversion despite ample shear being present for storm \norganization.\n\nThe second, and much higher, chance for storms is on Monday and \nMonday night when clusters of storms/MCSs may form to our northwest \nahead of an approaching cold front before diving south into the Ohio \nValley. Precise convective details are highly uncertain since the \nbackground environment over the region will likely be characterized \nby moderate to high instability and sufficient shear for organized \nconvection with little in the way of organized forcing. Storm to \nstorm interactions and lingering outflow boundaries from antecedent \nconvection will have large impacts on how Monday/Monday night plays \nout. At the time, the most likely timing for storms over the Ohio \nValley is Monday night. However, convection that occurs on Sunday \nand Sunday night could easily impact the forecast during the day on \nMonday. A damaging wind threat exists given the potential for \norganized clusters of storms/MCSs Monday into Monday night.\n\nIn addition, hot and humid conditions on Monday could lead to the \nheat index reaching the 95-100 degree range if convection does not \noccur early in the day.",
